window.__hvBdRegister("livy-2", {"key":"livy-2","sections":[{"i":0,"j":11,"loc":"Book VI 1–4","title":"Rome rebuilt and Camillus wins three wars","summary":"Livy opens his second start: the earlier history was obscure because records perished when the Gauls burned the City, and from here he promises a clearer account. Camillus remains dictator, records and laws are searched out, and the Day of the Allia is declared ill-omened. When the Volsci, Etruscans, Latins and Hernici threaten together, Camillus is named dictator again; he burns the Volscian barricade near Lanuvium, takes Bolae from the Aequi, and retakes Sutrium on the very day it surrendered to the Etruscans. He triumphs over three wars, Veii's squatters are called back, the City is rebuilt within a year, and the Capitol receives a substructure of hewn stone.","ideas":["Livy's new beginning: clearer history after the Gallic fire destroyed earlier records","Camillus as the man who carries Rome through its recovery","Sutrium captured twice in one day","Rapid rebuilding of the City"],"terms":[{"t":"dies Alliensis (Day of the Allia)","d":"July 18, the anniversary of the Cremera and Allia disasters, on which no public or private business might be done."},{"t":"interregnum","d":"A gap between magistrates in which a patrician interrex holds office and conducts elections."}]},{"i":12,"j":26,"loc":"Book VI 5–10","title":"Camillus as consular tribune at Satricum and Sutrium","summary":"Tribunes of the plebs push for the Pomptine land, but the plebs are too busy building to care. Four new tribes bring the total to twenty-five. With Camillus a consular tribune, his colleagues willingly hand him the supreme command against Antium, and he divides the tasks of the state among them. At Satricum he restores his soldiers' nerve with a speech and personally leads the charge; a storm ends the battle, the Latins and Hernici go home, and Satricum is stormed. Diverted from Antium to Etruria, he relieves Sutrium and storms Nepete, executing the Etruscans and the Nepesini who betrayed the town; the Latins and Hernici give evasive answers about failing to send troops.","ideas":["Colleagues subordinate their authority to Camillus voluntarily","Camillus' personal leadership turns a demoralized army","Sutrium and Nepete are the gateways of Etruria","Growing suspicion of Latin and Hernican loyalty"],"terms":[{"t":"tribuni militum consulari potestate (consular tribunes)","d":"Military tribunes holding consular authority, elected in place of consuls in these years."},{"t":"ager Pomptinus (Pomptine land)","d":"Public land taken from the Volsci, which the tribunes wanted shared out to the plebs."}]},{"i":27,"j":37,"loc":"Book VI 11–16","title":"Manlius Capitolinus turns champion of debtors","summary":"Marcus Manlius, saviour of the Capitol, envies Camillus and becomes the first patrician to court the plebs, attacking debt rather than only proposing land laws. The senate names Aulus Cornelius Cossus dictator; Livy pauses to wonder where the endlessly beaten Volsci found soldiers, and Cossus routs them in the Pomptine district, finding Latin and Hernican nobles among the captives. In Rome Manlius pays off a decorated centurion's debt, auctions his own farm for debtors, and claims the patricians are hiding Gallic gold. Summoned before the dictator, he refuses to prove the charge and is imprisoned; many of the plebs go into mourning, and Cossus's triumph earns more hatred than glory.","ideas":["Manlius' jealousy of Camillus drives him to the plebs","Debt as a sharper goad than land","The claim of hidden Gallic gold","The plebs submit to the dictator's power yet mourn Manlius"],"terms":[{"t":"nexum (debt bondage)","d":"The binding over of a defaulting debtor's person to his creditor, which Manlius sets out to break."},{"t":"dictator","d":"A single magistrate with supreme authority named for an emergency, here used against sedition at home."}]},{"i":38,"j":46,"loc":"Book VI 17–20","title":"The fall and death of Marcus Manlius","summary":"The crowd reproaches itself for abandoning its champions, as with Spurius Cassius and Spurius Maelius, and the senate releases Manlius, which only gives the sedition a leader. The Velitran colonists are harshly sent away. Manlius holds night meetings in his house on the Citadel and urges the plebs to count their numbers and level the dictatorship and consulship; Livy says the tradition is unclear on how far his kingly plans went. Two plebeian tribunes propose turning the plebs into his judges on a charge of seeking kingship. At trial Manlius displays his decorations and points to the Capitol, so the assembly is moved to the Peteline Wood out of its sight; he is condemned, thrown from the Tarpeian Rock, and his house site and praenomen are banned, and a plague follows.","ideas":["Manlius' speech urging the plebs to use their strength","Charge of aiming at kingship (regnum)","The trial moved out of sight of the Capitol","The same rock marks his glory and his punishment"],"terms":[{"t":"regnum (kingly power)","d":"The charge of seeking to rule as a king, the most hated accusation in the Republic."},{"t":"Tarpeian Rock","d":"The cliff of the Capitol from which traitors were thrown; Manlius had repelled the Gauls there."}]},{"i":47,"j":57,"loc":"Book VI 21–26","title":"Camillus, Lucius Furius, and the peace of Tusculum","summary":"After plague and famine, revolts break out at Velitrae, Circeii, Lanuvium and Praeneste; Praeneste and the Volsci sack the colony of Satricum. The aged Camillus, tribune for the sixth time, wants to wait, but his young colleague Lucius Furius stirs the army against him and is granted the battle; the Romans fall into a feigned retreat and flee, until Camillus, lifted onto his horse, rallies them and Lucius Furius pleads with the cavalry, and the Volsci are routed. Camillus generously chooses Lucius as his lieutenant for the war against Tusculum, whose men had been among the prisoners. Tusculum meets the army in togas with its gates open and its life undisturbed; its dictator pleads in the senate, and the Tusculans receive peace and later citizenship.","ideas":["Youthful rashness against aged prudence","Camillus' magnanimity to the colleague who caused the defeat","Tusculum disarms Rome by peaceful submission","Tusculum granted peace and later citizenship"],"terms":[{"t":"auspicia (auspices)","d":"The right to consult the gods' will before acting, which Camillus says his campaigns held under him alone."}]},{"i":58,"j":68,"loc":"Book VI 27–34","title":"Debt crisis and wars with Praeneste and Latins","summary":"A censorship called to look into debt fails, and the tribunes charge that the senate is hiding how badly the plebs are indebted while wearing them out with wars; they block the levy. The Praenestines march on the Colline Gate, then camp at the Allia expecting its evil fortune; the dictator Titus Quinctius Cincinnatus routs them, takes nine towns and Praeneste, and dedicates the image of Jupiter Imperator. The Manlii blunder into an ambush against the Volsci, a debt moratorium buys a levy, and the Romans beat Latins and Volsci at Satricum; Antium surrenders, the Latins burn Satricum except for the temple of Mater Matuta, and seize Tusculum, which a Roman army recovers. At home debtors are handed over in person and the plebs no longer even stand for office.","ideas":["The Allia's ill omen turned against Rome's enemies","Debt relief traded for the levy","The voice from the temple of Mater Matuta","Plebeian leadership crushed by debt"],"terms":[{"t":"tributum (war-tax)","d":"The property tax for war, suspended along with debt judgments during the Volscian war."},{"t":"Jupiter Imperator","d":"The image Cincinnatus brought from Praeneste and set up on the Capitol with an inscription of his nine towns."}]},{"i":69,"j":80,"loc":"Book VI 34–38","title":"The Licinian-Sextian bills and five years of deadlock","summary":"The younger daughter of Marcus Fabius Ambustus, married to the plebeian Gaius Licinius Stolo, envies her sister when a consular tribune's lictor knocks at the door, and her father promises she will see such honours at home. Licinius and Lucius Sextius become tribunes of the plebs and propose debt relief, a 500-iugera limit on land, and the end of consular tribunes with one consul to be plebeian. Patricians use colleagues' vetoes, so the two tribunes block all curule elections for five years. While Velitrae is besieged they keep pressing, argue the consulship is the citadel of liberty, and add a bill for a mixed board of ten for the sacred books. Camillus, made dictator, threatens to lead the plebs off to war but abdicates; Livy weighs the reasons and prefers a flaw in the auspices.","ideas":["A small domestic slight sets off a great change","The three Licinian-Sextian rogations","Tribunes paralyse the state with the veto","Camillus' abdication and Livy's judgement of its cause"],"terms":[{"t":"rogatio (bill)","d":"A measure put to the people or plebs for their vote."},{"t":"intercessio (veto)","d":"A tribune's power to block an act, used here by tribunes against their own colleagues."},{"t":"iugerum","d":"A Roman land measure; the bill would let no one hold more than five hundred."}]},{"i":81,"j":92,"loc":"Book VI 39–42 and Summary","title":"Appius Claudius' speech and the first plebeian consul","summary":"Publius Manlius as dictator names a plebeian master of the horse. Licinius and Sextius tell the plebs they must pass all the bills together or lose them. Appius Claudius Crassus replies that this is a tyranny imposing consuls on the voters, and argues that the auspices belong only to patricians, so plebeian consuls would pollute the state's religion. Their tenth tribunate brings a mixed board of ten for sacred rites; Camillus, dictator a fifth time, defeats the Gauls on Alban soil, though Livy notes Claudius's version placing the Manlius duel here. The laws pass, Lucius Sextius becomes the first plebeian consul, and Camillus brokers the compromise of a patrician praetor and patrician aediles; the book's summary follows.","ideas":["All-or-nothing package of reforms","Appius' argument from the auspices","First plebeian consul elected","Concord through the patrician praetorship"],"terms":[{"t":"praetor","d":"A new patrician magistrate created to administer justice in the City, given as compensation for the plebeian consulship."},{"t":"decemviri sacris faciundis","d":"The board of ten in charge of sacred rites and books, half of them now plebeian."},{"t":"ludi maximi (Great Games)","d":"Games vowed to the gods, extended by a day to celebrate the reconciliation of the orders."}]},{"i":93,"j":103,"loc":"Book VII 1–6","title":"Plague, Camillus' death, stage plays and Curtius","summary":"Sextius is consul, and the patricians gain a praetor and curule aediles, though the aedileship is soon shared. A plague kills Camillus, whom Livy honours as Rome's second Founder after Romulus. To appease the gods Etruscan dancers are brought in, and Livy traces the rise of Roman drama from improvised verse to Livius' plotted plays and Atellan farces. A Tiber flood interrupts the games, and Lucius Manlius is named dictator to drive the ritual nail, but tries to wage war and resigns. Prosecuted for cruelty, including banishing his slow-speaking son to the farm, he is saved when the son, Titus, forces the tribune at knife-point to drop the case. A chasm opens in the Forum and Marcus Curtius rides into it fully armed, devoting himself.","ideas":["Camillus as second Founder","Origins of Roman stage drama","The ritual nail driven to mark the years","Titus Manlius' filial piety; Curtius' self-devotion"],"terms":[{"t":"lectisternium","d":"A banquet offered to the gods to appease their anger."},{"t":"histriones","d":"Professional actors, from the Etruscan word ister, player."},{"t":"devotio (self-devotion)","d":"Offering one's own life to the gods for the state, as Curtius does at the chasm."}]},{"i":104,"j":113,"loc":"Book VII 6–11","title":"Genucius' death and Torquatus' duel with the Gaul","summary":"The first plebeian consul to lead under his own auspices, Lucius Genucius, is ambushed and killed by the Hernici, and the patricians crow that the gods have avenged the auspices; Appius Claudius is made dictator. The legate Sulpicius saves the camp, and Appius beats the Hernici's picked cohorts in a hard fight, the cavalry fighting on foot. Ferentinum falls and Tibur closes its gates. With the Gauls at the Anio bridge, a huge Gaul challenges Rome's bravest; Titus Manlius, with the dictator's leave, kills him and takes his neck-chain, earning the name Torquatus. The Gauls go to Tibur and Campania, return, and are defeated at the Colline Gate; Poetelius triumphs over Gauls and Tiburtes, who mock his triumph.","ideas":["Patrician taunts after the plebeian consul's death","The Hernican picked cohorts","Manlius Torquatus' single combat","Battle before the Colline Gate in sight of families"],"terms":[{"t":"torques (neck-chain)","d":"The Gaul's chain taken by Titus Manlius, from which he and his descendants were named Torquatus."},{"t":"fetiales (fetials)","d":"Priests who formally demand redress and declare war."}]},{"i":114,"j":128,"loc":"Book VII 12–16","title":"Sulpicius' Gallic triumph and laws on interest","summary":"A Tiburtine night attack on Rome is beaten off. War is declared on Tarquinii, the Latins renew their treaty and send troops, and the dictator Gaius Sulpicius delays against Gauls near Pedum. His soldiers grow mutinous, and the chief centurion Sextus Tullius speaks for them demanding battle. Sulpicius mounts muleteers as sham cavalry on the hills and, after shaming Tullius' men into fighting, routs the Gauls and dedicates their gold on the Capitol. Fabius is beaten by Tarquinii, who sacrifice 307 Roman prisoners; tribes are added, a law on bribery passes, and interest is fixed at one per cent. Marcius takes Privernum, a tax on freeing slaves is voted in camp and assemblies away from Rome are banned, and Licinius Stolo is fined under his own land law.","ideas":["Soldiers demand battle through Sextus Tullius","Muleteers disguised as cavalry","Tarquinian sacrifice of Roman captives","Licinius Stolo convicted under his own law"],"terms":[{"t":"primus pilus (first centurion)","d":"The senior centurion of a legion; Tullius held this post for the seventh time."},{"t":"fenus unciarium","d":"The one-per-cent rate of interest fixed by the tribunes Duillius and Menenius."}]},{"i":129,"j":141,"loc":"Book VII 17–24","title":"First plebeian dictator and struggles over the consulship","summary":"Etruscan priests with snakes and torches panic a Roman army before shame turns it to victory. Gaius Marcius Rutulus becomes the first plebeian dictator and triumphs by vote of the people without the senate's consent. Patricians use interregna to elect two patrician consuls, and the plebs walk out of the elections. Tarquinii is punished with mass executions while Tibur and the Samnites are treated more gently. Caere pleads its old hospitality to Rome's sacred objects and Vestals and gets a hundred-year truce. Five commissioners settle debts fairly from the treasury; Marcius is elected the first plebeian censor; the plebeian consul Popilius defeats the Gauls from a hill and is wounded, and the dictator Lucius Furius Camillus restores two patrician consuls.","ideas":["The plebeian dictatorship and censorship","Patrician resistance to the Licinian law","Caere's ancient merits win pardon","The five debt commissioners"],"terms":[{"t":"lex Licinia (Licinian law)","d":"The statute requiring that one consul be plebeian, repeatedly evaded in these years."},{"t":"mensarii (bankers)","d":"Five commissioners who cleared debts by public loans or fair valuation of property."},{"t":"censor","d":"Magistrate who takes the census of citizens and property."}]},{"i":142,"j":151,"loc":"Book VII 25–28","title":"Valerius Corvus and the raven; Juno Moneta","summary":"Popilius triumphs, while the Gauls raid from the Alban hills, Greek fleets harry the coast, and the Latins refuse soldiers. Ten legions are raised, which Livy contrasts with Rome's present devotion to wealth and luxury. With Appius Claudius dead, the consul Camillus camps in the Pomptine district; when a Gaul challenges, the tribune Marcus Valerius fights him while a raven perches on his helmet and attacks the Gaul's face, and the Gauls are routed. Valerius, now Corvus, is elected consul at twenty-three; the Greeks, probably Sicilian tyrants, leave Italy. A treaty is made with Carthage, interest is halved, Satricum is taken and burned, and Lucius Furius vows a temple to Juno Moneta on the site of Manlius' house.","ideas":["Raven omen in the single combat","Corvus consul at twenty-three","First treaty with Carthage","Temple of Moneta on Manlius' house site"],"terms":[{"t":"Corvus","d":"Surname of Marcus Valerius, from the raven (corvus) that fought for him."},{"t":"Juno Moneta","d":"Goddess whose temple on the Citadel was vowed during the war with the Aurunci."}]},{"i":152,"j":158,"loc":"Book VII 29–31","title":"Campania appeals to Rome against the Samnites","summary":"Livy announces greater wars to come, with the Samnites, Pyrrhus and Carthage. The Samnites attack the Sidicini, who turn to Campania; the Campanians are beaten twice and shut up in Capua. Their envoys offer friendship and promise Capua's wealth and loyalty, pressing that Campania will strengthen either Rome or Samnium. The senate refuses to break its treaty with the Samnites, so the Campanians surrender themselves, their city and their gods to Rome. Bound now by honour to defend subjects, Rome asks the Samnites to spare Campania, but the Samnite magistrates order a raid on it in the envoys' sight.","ideas":["The Samnite war arises from outside quarrels","The Campanian speech for Rome's help","Surrender (deditio) turns allies into Roman possessions","Samnite defiance of the Roman envoys"],"terms":[{"t":"deditio (surrender)","d":"The giving up of a people and its property wholly into Roman power, making them Rome's to defend."},{"t":"foedus (treaty)","d":"The alliance with the Samnites that Rome will not break to help Campania."}]},{"i":159,"j":174,"loc":"Book VII 32–38","title":"First Samnite War: Gaurus, Decius, and Suessula","summary":"War is declared. At Mount Gaurus Valerius Corvus tells his men to follow his deeds, not his words, and that the consulship now goes to merit; after a long, stubborn battle the Samnites break, saying the Romans' blazing eyes terrified them. In Samnium the consul Cornelius is trapped in a defile until the tribune Publius Decius seizes a hill above the enemy, then breaks his detachment out at night through the sleeping Samnites and leads a dawn attack that takes their camp. Decius receives a golden crown, oxen and grass crowns. At Suessula Valerius takes the Samnite camp while their men are out foraging and slaughters the dispersed army. The Faliscans and Latins react, Carthage sends a golden crown, and garrisons winter in Campania.","ideas":["Valerius' speech: office won by merit","Decius' seizure of the hill and night breakout","Samnites as Rome's most stubborn foe yet","Carthage congratulates Rome"],"terms":[{"t":"corona graminea (grass crown)","d":"Honour given to one who rescues an army from siege, bestowed on Decius."},{"t":"hastati and principes","d":"The first and second lines of the legion, which Decius takes up the hill."}]},{"i":175,"j":188,"loc":"Book VII 38–42 and Summary","title":"The Capuan conspiracy and the soldiers' mutiny","summary":"The garrisons in pleasure-loving Capua plot to seize the city from the Campanians. The consul Marcius Rutulus delays them, then quietly discharges the plotters, who gather at Lautulae, march to the Alban country, and force the lame veteran Titus Quinctius to be their general. Valerius Corvus, made dictator, meets them on Roman soil and pleads for reconciliation, recalling Coriolanus; Quinctius urges his men to trust him, and they submit. Laws grant amnesty and protect soldiers from being struck off the rolls, and Salonius asks that the clause aimed at him pass for the sake of harmony. Livy reports other accounts, including laws against lending at interest, and says the authorities agree only that a sedition happened and was settled; Privernum raids Roman colonies, and the book's summary follows.","ideas":["Capua's luxury corrupts the garrison","Rutulus' ruse to break up the plot","Corvus' plea against civil bloodshed","Livy's sources disagree on the mutiny"],"terms":[{"t":"secessio (secession)","d":"Withdrawal of citizens from the state in protest, which the mutineers are promised impunity for."},{"t":"lex sacrata (law under penalty of devotion)","d":"A law whose violator is accursed; used for the military law protecting enrolled soldiers."}]},{"i":189,"j":205,"loc":"Book VIII, chs. 1–7","title":"The Latin revolt and the son of Manlius","summary":"After victories over Privernum and the Antiates, Rome renews its treaty with the Samnites and leaves them free to attack the Sidicini, who turn to the Latins and Campanians. Emboldened by Rome's ambiguous answer, the Latins plot war; their praetor Annius demands before the senate one consul and half the senate for Latium, and Titus Manlius Torquatus answers him with fury before Jupiter's statue. Annius falls on the temple steps, and war is declared. Encamped near Capua, both consuls dream that one side's general must devote himself, and they forbid all single fighting. When the consul's son kills the Tusculan Geminus Maecius in a duel against orders, his father has him beheaded before the army.","ideas":["Latin demand for equal partnership: one consul and half the senate","Annius' fall on the Capitol read as a sign of divine anger","the twin dream of a general devoted to the Manes and Earth","the 'orders of Manlius' as a lasting type of severity"],"terms":[{"t":"interrex","d":"A senator who briefly holds supreme authority to conduct an election when there are no consuls."},{"t":"imperium (consular authority)","d":"The power of command which Manlius says must be upheld even at the cost of his son's life."}]},{"i":206,"j":217,"loc":"Book VIII, chs. 8–12","title":"Battle near Vesuvius and the devotion of Decius","summary":"Livy describes the Roman legion of the time, with its lines of hastati, principes and triarii, and notes that the Latins fought in exactly the same formation. In the battle near Veseris the Roman left gives way, and Decius has the pontiff Marcus Valerius dictate the words of devotion, then rides into the enemy and dies, spreading panic among the Latins. Manlius holds back his triarii until the enemy's reserves are spent and then wins the day. Livy records the rules for devotion, and the Latins rally under Numisius only to be crushed at Trifanum. Latium and Capua lose land, the Campanian knights are rewarded, and the young men of Rome shun Manlius for the rest of his life.","ideas":["the manipular order and the saying 'to have come to the triarii'","Decius' self-devotion turns the battle","Manlius' skilful use of reserves","land confiscations and rewards after the Latin surrender"],"terms":[{"t":"devotio (devotion)","d":"A rite in which a commander vows himself and the enemy's legions to the Manes and Earth to win victory."},{"t":"triarii","d":"The veteran rear line of the legion, kneeling in reserve until the last stage of a battle."}]},{"i":218,"j":229,"loc":"Book VIII, chs. 12–17","title":"Publilian laws and the settlement of Latium","summary":"The consul Aemilius, refused a triumph, turns against the senate, and his colleague Publilius as dictator passes three laws favouring the plebs. The next consuls, Camillus and Maenius, take Pedum and subdue all Latium, and Camillus asks the senate whether to destroy the Latins or make them citizens. Each people is dealt with separately: some receive citizenship, Velitrae and Antium are punished, and the Antiate ships' beaks adorn the Rostra. Livy then records the Vestal Minucia's burial alive, Publilius as the first plebeian praetor, Valerius Corvus' capture of Cales, irregular dictators, and Alexander of Epirus' landing and victory in Lucania.","ideas":["the Publilian laws: plebiscites binding on all, prior senatorial ratification, a plebeian censor","Camillus' choice between cruelty and generosity toward the conquered","Latin peoples treated case by case; origin of the Rostra","Cales captured with help from an escaped prisoner"],"terms":[{"t":"civitas sine suffragio (citizenship without the vote)","d":"A grant of Roman citizenship without voting rights, given to the Campanians and others."},{"t":"Rostra","d":"The speaker's platform in the Forum, decorated with the beaks of captured Antiate warships."}]},{"i":230,"j":240,"loc":"Book VIII, chs. 18–22","title":"The poisoning scandal and the Privernate war","summary":"In a year of pestilence, a slave-woman reveals that matrons are preparing poisons; twenty drink their own drugs and die, and a hundred and seventy are convicted, so a dictator is named to drive the nail. Vitruvius Vaccus of Fundi leads Privernum against Rome and is defeated, while the Fundanian senate disowns the war. Privernum is taken, Vitruvius is executed and his house razed, and the consul Plautius asks the senate what to do with the people. A Privernate envoy's proud reply about free men divides the senate, but the merciful side prevails, holding that only those who value liberty deserve to be Romans, and the Privernates receive citizenship.","ideas":["the first poisoning trial at Rome, treated as a prodigy","Fundi pleads loyalty and is spared","the Privernate's answer: a good peace will be kept, a bad one will not","citizenship granted to those who prize liberty"],"terms":[{"t":"driving the nail","d":"An old rite of expiation performed by a dictator in times of crisis."}]},{"i":241,"j":252,"loc":"Book VIII, chs. 22–28","title":"Palaepolis, Alexander of Epirus, and debt bondage ended","summary":"The Greeks of Palaepolis attack Romans in Campania, and Publilius besieges them while the Samnites defy Rome and call for war in the Campanian plain. Publilius' command is extended so he can finish the siege, the first such prorogation, and the city is betrayed to him by Charilaus and Nymphius, who trick the Samnite garrison out of the walls. Livy tells how Alexander of Epirus, fleeing an oracle about Pandosia and the Acheron, is killed crossing a river of that name. The Tarentines provoke the Lucanians into abandoning Rome, and a creditor's cruelty to a young debtor ends imprisonment for debt.","ideas":["the first extension of command and triumph after office","the betrayal of Palaepolis by its leading citizens","Alexander of Epirus meets the fate he sought to escape","debtors' persons no longer pledged for loans"],"terms":[{"t":"proconsul","d":"A former consul whose command is extended past his year of office, first granted to Publilius Philo."},{"t":"fetials","d":"Priests who demand redress and declare war according to sacred procedure."}]},{"i":253,"j":268,"loc":"Book VIII, chs. 29–35","title":"Papirius Cursor and his master of horse","summary":"War is declared on the Vestini, and Brutus takes their towns while the dictator Papirius Cursor commands in Samnium. Returning to Rome to renew the auspices, Papirius forbids his master of the horse, Quintus Fabius, to fight, but Fabius wins a great victory at Imbrinium and burns the spoils. Papirius comes back intending to execute him; Fabius appeals to the soldiers, escapes to Rome, and his father appeals to the people. Papirius argues at length that military discipline will collapse if disobedience goes unpunished, and finally grants Fabius' life to the people's entreaties while declaring him guilty.","ideas":["victory gained against the dictator's orders","the clash between the dictator's authority and the appeal to the people","Papirius' speech on the collapse of discipline","pardon granted as a gift to the people, not as acquittal"],"terms":[{"t":"magister equitum (master of the horse)","d":"The dictator's second-in-command, bound to obey him."},{"t":"provocatio (appeal)","d":"A citizen's appeal to the people against a magistrate's sentence."}]},{"i":269,"j":281,"loc":"Book VIII, chs. 36–40, and Summary","title":"Samnite defeats and the surrender of Brutulus","summary":"Papirius wins back his soldiers by visiting the wounded and defeats the Samnites, who obtain a year's truce and soon break it. The Tusculans are nearly condemned by the Flavian bill but are saved by their supplications. The dictator Cornelius Arvina, caught in a bad position, fights a long, even battle until the Samnite cavalry turn to plundering his baggage and are cut down. The Samnites blame the broken treaty and surrender the dead body of Papius Brutulus with Roman prisoners and booty. Livy notes how family eulogies and false inscriptions have corrupted the records; the book's summary follows.","ideas":["Papirius tempers sternness with kindness","the Samnite horse caught plundering the baggage","Samnite atonement through the body of Brutulus","Livy's doubts about family records and inscriptions"],"terms":[]},{"i":282,"j":298,"loc":"Book IX, chs. 1–7","title":"The Caudine Forks","summary":"Gaius Pontius tells the Samnites their war is now just, since Rome rejected their atonement. Using false reports that Luceria is under siege, he draws the consuls Veturius and Postumius into the Caudine Forks, where both exits are blocked. His father Herennius advises either releasing the Romans unharmed or killing them all, warning that a middle course will make no friends and remove no enemies, but he is not heeded. Persuaded by Lucius Lentulus that the army is the country, the consuls make a pledge of peace, give six hundred knights as hostages, and pass under the yoke. The army is received kindly at Capua but returns to Rome in silent shame, and Rome goes into mourning.","ideas":["the trap in the enclosed valley","Herennius Pontius' two choices","Lentulus: saving the army saves the country","the passage under the yoke and the shame at Rome"],"terms":[{"t":"sponsio (guarantee)","d":"A personal pledge by the commanders, which Livy distinguishes from a treaty ratified by the people."},{"t":"the yoke","d":"An arch of spears under which the defeated were made to pass unarmed as a sign of submission."}]},{"i":299,"j":307,"loc":"Book IX, chs. 8–12","title":"Postumius gives himself up to the Samnites","summary":"In the senate Spurius Postumius proposes that he and the other guarantors be surrendered, stripped and bound, since the people never authorised the pledge and so are not bound by it. He answers the tribunes who object, and the tribunes resign so that they can be handed over too. At Caudium the fetial delivers them, and Postumius knocks the fetial with his knee, declaring himself a Samnite who has struck an envoy. Pontius refuses the surrender, calls it a childish evasion, and says Rome must either keep the peace or put the legions back in the pass. The Samnites now regret ignoring Herennius' advice.","ideas":["a pledge given without the people's mandate binds only those who gave it","Postumius' surrender compared to Decius' devotion","Pontius denounces Roman legal tricks","Rome now feels sure of winning the renewed war"],"terms":[{"t":"fetial law","d":"The sacred rules for declaring war and handing over those who broke faith."}]},{"i":308,"j":317,"loc":"Book IX, chs. 12–16","title":"Revenge at Luceria and Papirius Cursor","summary":"The Samnites take Fregellae by a false promise of safety and burn those who surrender. Publilius routs the Samnites, and Papirius besieges Luceria, brushes aside Tarentine envoys who threaten whichever side refuses peace, and storms the Samnite camp. Luceria is starved into surrender, seven thousand Samnites pass under the yoke, and the six hundred hostages and lost standards are recovered. Livy notes doubts about which commanders won these victories, tells of the capture of Satricum, and describes Papirius Cursor's strength, speed, harshness and command, ranking him a match for Alexander.","ideas":["the hostage knights keep the soldiers' vengeance in check","the Samnites sent under the yoke at Luceria","Satricum betrayed by both of its factions","Papirius' character and dry wit"],"terms":[]},{"i":318,"j":324,"loc":"Book IX, chs. 17–19","title":"Livy's digression: Rome against Alexander","summary":"Livy breaks off his narrative to ask how Rome would have fared against Alexander the Great. He weighs commanders, numbers, troops and fortune. Alexander owed much of his fame to dying young and undefeated and to fighting soft Asian enemies, and he was being corrupted by Persian ways. Rome had many generals his equal, a senate of kings, deep reserves of manpower and allies, and a more flexible line and better arms. Livy concludes that a single lost battle would have ruined Alexander, whereas neither Caudium nor Cannae overthrew Rome.","ideas":["one young man's ten years against a people's four centuries","the yearly-elected Roman commanders compared with an unhindered king","the legion against the phalanx","Rome never beaten by infantry in open battle"],"terms":[{"t":"Fortuna (Fortune)","d":"One of the factors of war Livy weighs, which a short life exposes less to reversal."}]},{"i":325,"j":336,"loc":"Book IX, chs. 20–26","title":"Saticula, Sora, and the inquiry into conspiracies","summary":"The Samnites get a two-year truce, Apulia submits, and prefects are sent to Capua as Roman law spreads. War resumes at Saticula; the master of the horse Aulius dies in a cavalry fight, and Fabius as dictator burns his own camp to force his men to win. Sora is taken through a deserter's trick, the Ausones are wiped out by betrayal, and Luceria is retaken and colonised. A dictator, Maenius, investigates conspiracies at Capua and then at Rome; when the nobles accuse him, he resigns and is acquitted in trial, and the inquiry later fades.","ideas":["Roman law adopted at Capua and Antium","Fabius burns his camp to cut off retreat","Sora's citadel taken by ten men and a deserter","Maenius resigns to stand trial as a private citizen"],"terms":[{"t":"praefectus (prefect)","d":"A Roman official sent to administer justice at Capua at the Capuans' request."}]},{"i":337,"j":350,"loc":"Book IX, chs. 27–33","title":"Samnite defeat, Appius' censorship, and Etruscan war","summary":"The consuls Poetelius and Sulpicius defeat the Samnites near Caudium, killing or capturing about thirty thousand, and Fregellae and Nola are recovered. As an Etruscan war looms, the censor Appius Claudius builds his road and aqueduct, packs the senate, and transfers the rites of Hercules to public slaves, after which the Potitii die out and he is later struck blind. The next consuls ignore his senate list, the people begin to elect military tribunes, and the flute-players who fled to Tibur are brought back drunk in wagons. Junius takes Cluviae and Bovianum and escapes a Samnite ambush, while Aemilius fights a bloody, indecisive battle at Sutrium.","ideas":["Appius' public works and his abuse of the senate list","the end of the Potitii read as divine punishment","the flute-players' strike and its comic ending","the opening of the great Etruscan war at Sutrium"],"terms":[{"t":"censor","d":"Magistrate who took the census, revised the senate roll and let public contracts."}]},{"i":351,"j":362,"loc":"Book IX, chs. 33–38","title":"Appius defies the law; Fabius crosses the Ciminian Forest","summary":"The tribune Sempronius charges Appius Claudius with keeping the censorship beyond the eighteen months set by the Aemilian law, listing his family's record of arrogance, and orders him to prison; three tribunes protect him and he stays on alone. The consul Fabius defeats the Etruscans at Sutrium, and his brother, who knows the Etruscan language, scouts through the dreaded Ciminian Forest disguised as a shepherd and wins over the Umbrian Camertes. Fabius crosses the forest and plunders Etruria before the senate's order forbidding it arrives. He then surprises a huge Etruscan army asleep at dawn, and Perusia, Cortona and Arretium obtain a thirty-year truce.","ideas":["the later law overrides the earlier","the Claudii as enemies of plebeian liberty","the scouting of the Ciminian Forest","the dawn attack that crushes the Etruscans"],"terms":[{"t":"lex Aemilia (Aemilian law)","d":"The law limiting the censorship to eighteen months."}]},{"i":363,"j":376,"loc":"Book IX, chs. 38–41","title":"Papirius' dictatorship and Samnites in gilded armour","summary":"A Roman fleet's raid near Nuceria ends badly, and the consul Marcius is wounded in a hard battle with the Samnites. The senate needs Papirius as dictator, and Fabius, his old enemy, names him in silence at night. The Etruscans, under a sacred levy, fight more fiercely than ever but are finally broken. Papirius defeats the Samnites, whose corps in gold and silver armour are scorned as spoil rather than arms; their shields decorate the Forum, and Campanian gladiators take the name Samnites. Fabius beats the Etruscans at Perusia and then the Umbrians at Mevania, whose men are dragged from their ranks and surrender during the battle.","ideas":["Fabius sets aside his private grudge for the state","the Faucian curia as an evil omen","iron and courage against gold and silver","Umbrian threat against Rome ended at Mevania"],"terms":[{"t":"lex sacrata","d":"A sacred oath-bound levy in which each soldier chose a comrade, used by the Etruscans."},{"t":"curia Faucia","d":"A voting ward whose first vote was held unlucky, recalling the Gallic capture and the Caudine Peace."}]},{"i":377,"j":395,"loc":"Book IX, chs. 42–46, and Summary","title":"End of the Second Samnite War and Gnaeus Flavius","summary":"Fabius as proconsul defeats the Samnites at Allifae, and Hernici found among the captives lead the Hernici to revolt, but Marcius soon subdues them. The consuls crush the Samnites, Bovianum falls, and the Samnites' old treaty is restored. The Aequi are punished, losing thirty-one towns in fifty days, and several peoples ask for alliance. Gnaeus Flavius, a freedman's son, is elected curule aedile, publishes the civil law formulae and the calendar, and defies the nobles. Fabius as censor puts the market-place crowd into four urban tribes and is called Maximus; the book's summary closes the range.","ideas":["Hernic peoples rewarded or punished by their loyalty","the Aequi destroyed for refusing Roman terms","Flavius makes legal procedure public","the urban tribes and Fabius' surname Maximus"],"terms":[{"t":"aedile","d":"Magistrate in charge of city affairs; Flavius held the curule aedileship despite his humble birth."},{"t":"tribus urbanae (urban tribes)","d":"Four tribes into which Fabius placed the city's lowly voters to limit their influence."}]},{"i":396,"j":406,"loc":"Book X, ch. 1–5","title":"Cleonymus, the Marsi, and the Etruscan rising","summary":"Book X opens with colonies at Sora and Alba, citizenship for the Arpinates and Trebulani, and the smoking out of brigands from an Umbrian cave. The dictator Junius Bubulcus subdues the Aequi, and the Spartan Cleonymus, driven from the Sallentine coast, sails up the Adriatic and is routed by the Patavians among the lagoons of the Veneti. The dictator Marcus Valerius Maximus defeats the Marsi and then the Etruscans, after his master of the horse loses standards in an ambush. At Rusellae a feigned flock of shepherds is exposed and the Etruscans are broken by a cavalry charge through lanes left in the Roman line; they win a two-year truce, and Valerius becomes consul.","ideas":["Patavium still displays the beaks of Cleonymus' ships and holds a yearly boat contest","Livy doubts annalists who make Fabius master of the horse, blaming the surname Maximus","the refined speech and looks of false shepherds betray an Etruscan ambush","the dictator holds back his men until the Etruscans tire, then lets the cavalry loose"],"terms":[{"t":"magister equitum (master of the horse)","d":"The dictator's deputy, here blamed for a defeat suffered while the dictator was in Rome retaking the auspices."},{"t":"iustitium (cessation of legal business)","d":"The closing of the courts proclaimed at Rome in an emergency, as after the reverse in Etruria."}]},{"i":407,"j":418,"loc":"Book X, ch. 6–11","title":"The Ogulnian law opens the priesthoods","summary":"In a quiet year the tribunes Quintus and Gnaeus Ogulnius propose to add four pontiffs and five augurs, all plebeian. Appius Claudius opposes the bill and Publius Decius Mus defends it, invoking his father's self-sacrifice and the plebeian consuls, dictators and triumphs already won; he argues that patricians were simply freeborn men who could name their fathers. The law passes and the new priests are named; Marcus Valerius renews the law of appeal, and Nequinum is betrayed through a tunnel and becomes the colony Narnia. The Etruscans buy off a Gallic host that then refuses to fight without land, the consul Titus Manlius dies after a fall from his horse, and the Picentes warn that the Samnites are arming.","ideas":["plebeian leaders who hold consulships now seek the priesthoods as well","Decius: plebeian auspices have prospered as well as patrician ones","the Valerian law of appeal only declared a breach a wicked act, which Livy contrasts with the Porcian law","the Gauls take Etruscan money but demand land before fighting Rome"],"terms":[{"t":"augures and pontifices","d":"The two great priestly colleges, raised by the Ogulnian law to nine augurs and eight pontiffs with plebeian members."},{"t":"provocatio (appeal)","d":"A citizen's right to appeal to the people against a magistrate's sentence of scourging or death."}]},{"i":419,"j":439,"loc":"Book X, ch. 11–17","title":"War renewed with Samnium; Fabius and Decius consuls","summary":"The Lucanians ask for protection against Samnite invasion, Rome makes a treaty with them, and war is declared when the Samnites threaten the fetials. Scipio fights an indecisive battle near Volaterrae and ravages Etruria, while Fulvius wins at Bovianum. Fabius, though not a candidate and pleading age and the ten-year law, is elected consul with Decius; at Tifernum he beats the Samnites by sending hastati over the mountains and crying that Decius is coming, while Decius defeats the Apulians. Fabius refuses to preside over his own election, and Appius Claudius and Volumnius become consuls; the Samnite army leaves for Etruria, and Decius storms Murgantia, Romulea and Ferentinum.","ideas":["Fabius asks why make laws if their makers break them, yet yields to the people","a detachment appearing on the heights is taken for the second consular army","Samnite envoys urge the Etruscans to drive Rome back across the Tiber","Decius has his men sell their booty so they can march on to richer towns"],"terms":[{"t":"fetiales (fetials)","d":"Priests who demanded redress from another people and declared war in due form."},{"t":"hastati","d":"The front line of the legion, here sent on a hidden detour to fall on the Samnite rear."}]},{"i":440,"j":452,"loc":"Book X, ch. 18–22","title":"Appius and Volumnius quarrel and win together","summary":"The Samnite Gellius Egnatius raises a coalition of Etruscans, Umbrians and Gauls, and Appius Claudius makes little headway against it. Volumnius arrives saying he was summoned by letter; Appius denies sending one, and only the officers and soldiers keep the consuls together, shouting for Volumnius to stay. In the battle that follows Appius vows a temple to Bellona and the joint armies take the enemy camp. Volumnius then surprises a Samnite army laden with Campanian plunder, its own prisoners rising and seizing the general Staius Minatius; Rome, in panic, founds Minturnae and Sinuessa, and Volumnius secures the election of Fabius and Decius, with Appius named praetor.","ideas":["the quarrel between the consuls is settled by the soldiers' shout","Appius' vow to Bellona marks the turn of the battle","freed prisoners carry off the Samnite general horse and all","Volumnius says the eloquent like Appius belong in the courts, soldiers in the consulship"],"terms":[{"t":"Bellona","d":"Roman goddess of war, to whom Appius vows a temple in the heat of battle."},{"t":"prorogatio (extension of command)","d":"Prolonging a magistrate's command beyond his year, granted here to Fabius, Decius and Volumnius."}]},{"i":453,"j":463,"loc":"Book X, ch. 23–26","title":"Plebeian Modesty and the Etruscan command","summary":"Verginia, a patrician married to the plebeian consul Volumnius, is shut out of the shrine of Patrician Modesty and founds an altar of Plebeian Modesty in her house; the Ogulnii adorn the Capitol and set up the wolf and twins from usurers' fines. Fabius and Decius dispute whether Etruria should go to Fabius without lot, Decius complaining that the Fathers are taking from the people what belongs to them; the people award it to Fabius. Fabius marches with a small army, tells Appius' wood-gatherers to pull up their own stockade, and returns to Rome, where he asks for Decius as his colleague in the war. Meanwhile Senonian Gauls cut off a Roman legion near Clusium, in Livy's judgement more likely than the Umbrians whom some writers name.","ideas":["the cult of Plebeian Modesty admits only matrons married to one man","Fabius: one man plants the tree, another should not pick its fruit","Fabius keeps his army on the move rather than in a fixed camp","Gallic horsemen are said to have ridden up with heads on their lances"],"terms":[{"t":"Pudicitia Patricia / Plebeia","d":"Patrician and Plebeian Modesty, rival cults of chaste matrons, the second founded by Verginia."},{"t":"sortitio (casting lots)","d":"The usual way consuls divided their provinces, which Decius insists on and the senate sets aside."}]},{"i":464,"j":477,"loc":"Book X, ch. 27–31","title":"Sentinum and the devotion of Decius","summary":"At Sentinum the consuls lure the Etruscans and Umbrians home by ravaging their lands and fight the Samnites and Gauls alone; a hind killed by the Gauls and a wolf spared by the Romans are read as omens. Fabius on the right holds back to wear the enemy down, while Decius on the left spends his strength, and his cavalry is routed by Gallic chariots. Decius then devotes himself and the enemy legions to Earth and the Manes in his father's words and dies charging the Gauls; the Romans rally, Fabius routs the Samnites, Gellius Egnatius falls, and the camp is taken. Fabius triumphs, the Samnites are defeated again near Caiatia, and Livy reflects on a people who would rather be conquered than not try for victory.","ideas":["Fabius judges that Samnites and Gauls fight hardest at the start and then fade","Decius repeats his father's devotio and the battle turns","the Gauls' locked shields are broken with javelins gathered from the field","Livy rejects exaggerated figures of 600,000 enemy infantry"],"terms":[{"t":"devotio (self-devotion)","d":"A rite in which a general offers himself and the enemy army to the gods below to win the battle."},{"t":"testudo","d":"A tightly packed formation behind interlocked shields, used here by the Gauls."},{"t":"Manes","d":"The spirits of the dead, to whom Decius consigns himself and the enemy."}]},{"i":478,"j":494,"loc":"Book X, ch. 32–37","title":"Postumius and Atilius: Luceria and a disputed triumph","summary":"Under the consuls Postumius and Atilius the Samnites break into Atilius' camp under cover of fog and kill the quaestor before being thrown out. Postumius storms Milionia and finds Feritrum and its neighbours abandoned, while Atilius, after a costly battle near Luceria, has to drive his own fleeing men back from the camp gate and vows a temple to Jupiter the Stayer before winning. He destroys a Samnite raiding army near Interamna but is denied a triumph for his losses and for sending prisoners under the yoke. Postumius wins in Etruria, grants Volsinii, Perusia and Arretium a forty-year truce, and triumphs against the senate with three tribunes' support; Livy notes that the annalists disagree about the year.","ideas":["despair makes the Samnites bold enough to assault a Roman camp","Atilius posts cavalry at the gate to treat all comers as enemies","Postumius claims a triumph on his own authority and the people's will","the vow to Jupiter Stator echoes Romulus' vow"],"terms":[{"t":"Iuppiter Stator (Jupiter the Stayer)","d":"Jupiter as the god who halts a flight, to whom Atilius vows a temple."},{"t":"sub iugum (under the yoke)","d":"Sending defeated men beneath a frame of spears as a humiliation, held against Atilius since no terms had been made."}]},{"i":495,"j":503,"loc":"Book X, ch. 38–41","title":"The Linen Legion and the battle of Aquilonia","summary":"Facing Lucius Papirius Cursor, son of the great Papirius, the Samnites muster at Aquilonia and swear their nobles in a linen-roofed enclosure, under a dread oath, to fight to the death; those who refuse are beheaded, and sixteen thousand form the Linen Legion. Carvilius takes Amiternum and besieges Cominium while Papirius camps before Aquilonia, telling his men that crests and gilded shields wound no one. When the sacred chickens will not feed their keeper reports a good omen; Papirius places him in the front rank, a javelin kills him, and a raven's cry confirms the gods' favour. The Samnites, held in place by fear of their oath, are routed when Spurius Nautius' mule-drivers raise dust like an army and Papirius claims that Cominium has fallen.","ideas":["the Samnite oath binds men to kill anyone who flees","Papirius holds that a false omen falls on the man who reports it","the Samnites fear their own countrymen more than the enemy","dragged branches make a few auxiliaries look like a great host"],"terms":[{"t":"Legio linteata (Linen Legion)","d":"The Samnite elite sworn under a linen canopy and given splendid arms and crested helmets."},{"t":"pullarius (keeper of the chickens)","d":"The attendant who took the auspices from the feeding of sacred chickens."}]},{"i":504,"j":521,"loc":"Book X, ch. 42–47; Summary of Book X","title":"Aquilonia and Cominium fall; the triumphs","summary":"Aquilonia is taken and abandoned in the night, and Papirius' vow of a cup of mead to Jupiter Victor is recorded; Carvilius storms Cominium the same day, while the Samnite cohorts sent between the two towns are routed at dawn. Both towns are burned, the consuls decorate their men and decide to stay until Samnium is crushed, taking Saepinum, Velia, Palumbinum and Herculaneum. War is declared on the Faliscans, Carvilius moves to Etruria and takes Troilum, and both consuls triumph; Papirius puts all his spoil into the Treasury and dedicates the temple of Quirinus, while Carvilius gives his soldiers a share. The book ends with the census, crowns and palms at the games, and a plague for which the Sibylline Books bid Aesculapius be brought from Epidaurus, followed by the summary of Book X.","ideas":["Papirius' parsimony with the booty angers the plebs","Carvilius builds a temple of Fors Fortuna from his spoils","palms for victors are a custom borrowed from the Greeks","the plague leads to the call for Aesculapius from Epidaurus"],"terms":[{"t":"triumphus (triumph)","d":"The victory procession through Rome, celebrated here by both consuls."},{"t":"lustrum","d":"The purification closing the census, which counted 262,321 citizens in the text (272,320 in the summary)."},{"t":"Libri Sibyllini (Sibylline Books)","d":"Oracle books consulted in crises, which here direct that Aesculapius be summoned."}]}]});